A||N||6||fastonpumpgas||New Fuel regulator ????||||5
Z||000000||fastonpumpgas||10-29-1999||03:43 PM||||    I was wondering about if i need a new fuel regulator for my 3.2 ??  I am still puttingthe motor together, but I would like to change this b4 it goes in, I guess it wouldnt hurt to get a adjustable fuel regulator, But is it needed ??<P>    This may help ;  The motor has a 250 grind cam , 9 to 1 compression, ported heads and exhaust manifolds,  I dont know if any of this stuff plays into it or not, thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||10-29-1999||03:57 PM||||I'd replace it if it's hard to get to. It's not worth the trouble to rip it apart again latter. whatever it costs you now it will cost more latter, especially when you add another set of intake gaskets etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Stroker||10-31-1999||06:14 PM||||fastonpumpgas,<BR>         You DEFINITELY need an adjustable pressure regulator.When increasing displacement your engine will need more fuel in concert with the higher volume of air drawn into the cylinders.<BR>  You can obtain one from Summit Racing or Accel.The part in either case is Accel part # 74750.<BR>  When you do have it running, you may want to start out at around 46-48 psi on the fuel rail WITHOUT cranking.Just attach your fuel pressure gauge to the schrader valve on the fuel rail and turn the key to the ON or RUN position but don't crank the engine.The reading that you get just after you hear the fuel pump stop is the one you're after.(46-48psi)<BR>  You can then experiment from here, but an easier way to be right on the money is to invest in an air/fuel ratio gauge that connects to your existing O2 sensor.This way you can "see" inside the cylinders by watching the gauge.This is an invaluable tool for optimum perfomance tuning and for higher compression ratios running alot of advance, it can keep you from running lean under load.<BR> Have fun, and keep us posted!<P>               Stroker||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||11-01-1999||08:09 AM||||Will that Accel fuel regulator also work with the turbo that I am going to install on my 3.1 rebuild?  I have seen a reference to a Bell Engineering (very expensive) fuel regulator and was wondering if the ACCEL part # would be an acceptable replacement.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Stroker||11-01-1999||06:39 PM||||BOILERMAKER,<BR>          The fuel pressure reg you saw from Bell is a rising rate reg.That's different from a regular reg in that it is put in line on the fuel return line and is used to hold the pressure higher under boost conditions.The one from Accel replaces the original on the fuel rail.You'll need both of them or you can use a reprogramed PROM instead of the Bell-type unit.Either way you still need the adjustable reg on the fuel rail.<BR>            Stroker||||reg||1
Z||000005||BOILERMAKER||11-02-1999||07:49 AM||||Good info, I didn't know that.  If I wanted to go with the programmed Prom, where would I go, what would I say to whomever I talk to (how would I order the Prom), and would I also need to get the accel # fuel regulator to go along with the Prom?||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||11-02-1999||09:43 AM||||If you get a new prom to increase the fuel, you don't need a variable FP regulator. <P>In closed loop, the ECM will be able to adjust the BLM to get it right. Just watch that it doesn't go past about 140 or so. 128 is ideal. <P>At WOT, watch the O2, if it drops below .8V, then a chip or more pressure will be needed.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-02-1999).]||||reg||1
